From 36b9ed9b6a00430c1afb0ecde756a322d812af32 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 09:04:39 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 077/119] scene-referred S3: the luminance weights follow the
 buffer, and the clamp audit comes back empty

GetLuminance() is dot(colour, (0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722)) - the Rec.709 Y row.
Applied to ACEScg data it is not luminance. Red loses about 28 % of what it is
worth, so a saturated red lamp reads dimmer than it is, sits below the glare
threshold, and spec S3's own acceptance case ("a saturated red light bloomed and
pushed +2 stops stays red") fails silently. Every threshold, every hue-preserving
rescale and the metered EV move with it.

CBPerViewGlobal gains CV_SceneLuminanceWeightsDelta beside CV_SceneExposure, and
CommonMath.cfi gains GetWorkingLuminanceWeights() / GetWorkingLuminance() on top of
it. A per-view CONSTANT and not a static flag, deliberately: the DOF gather
evaluates this inside a 121-tap loop and the firefly filter thirteen times per
texel, and a flag would double the permutation count of every one of those
techniques to carry three floats. The compiler hoists the fetch out of both loops.

Stored as the DELTA from Rec.709 rather than as the weights, which is the same trick
CV_SceneExposure uses and matters more here: a pass that does not bind the per-view
buffer would read (0,0,0) and compute a luminance of zero. As a delta it reads
(0,0,0) and gets the stock answer. On the stock path the delta is an exact 0.0, so
the addition is bit-identical.

A separate name and not a switch inside GetLuminanceWeights(): two thirds of that
function's call sites read the G-buffer, do shading-time energy conservation, or run
after the display transform, and Rec.709 is still right in all of them.

Sites moved, all of them between the conversion point and the tone map:
  PostEffects.cfx:305-353 + :356-364   the stable downsample's firefly reweight,
                                        13 taps and 5 inverses
  HDRPostProcess.cfx:750               the exposure meter - the EV-defining site
  HDRPostProcess.cfx:833               the tone map's saturation pivot
  DepthOfField.cfx:435                 bokeh splat highlight extraction
  DepthOfField.cfx:756                 ghost source extraction
  DepthOfField.cfx:1105-1109           the gather/splat hand-over - same weights as
                                        :435 or the two stop summing to the original
                                        energy

Explicitly NOT moved: HDRPostProcess.cfx:743 and :746. The audit lists them next to
:750, but they read the G-BUFFER - albedo and reflectance never enter the working
space. Also not moved: SMAA's luma edge detection, the photo filter, the cloud and
fog reprojection tonemaps (they run before the conversion point) and SSR's limiter
(it reads Prev, which stays 709). And NOT the glare threshold constants - that is
D11 / Stage S7, and mixing a primaries change with a re-thresholding makes the
screen unreadable.

Two passes had never bound the per-view buffer and now do: CStableDownsamplePass
and CAutoExposureStage's initial luminance pass. The tone map, both DOF splat
primitives and the layer downscale already did.

The gate is a third predicate, CRendererResources::IsSceneReferredWorkingSpace() =
IsSceneReferred() && r_SceneReferredDebug != 1. It has to be: with the debug A/B
skipping the conversion, IsSceneReferred() alone would leave Rec.709 data being
measured with AP1 weights, and the A/B would compare two different mistakes.

The other half of this item - lift every colour-data saturate/clamp between the HDR
target and the tone map - was walked site by site and comes back EMPTY. Every
candidate is already handled somewhere else: the R11G11B10F copies by S0's fp16
floor, the shafts' UNORM wall by S2, the glare soft knee is a threshold and belongs
to S7, :838's max(c,0) is harmless while 709 is a subset of AP1 and belongs to S4
with the CDL, and :841/:844/:871 are the display wall itself. Our own effects clamp
nothing but masks - PostEffects.cfx:295-370 contains not one saturate, clamp, min or
max - which is the lens model having been written scene-referred from the start.
Recorded in research/s3-luminance-and-saturates.md section 9 rather than left as an
absence, along with the one thing still worth watching: the per-channel max
accumulations in the DOF gather are legal but not basis-invariant.

Build: CryRenderD3D11, CinematicCamera, 0/0.

